  {
   "title": "Six ladder-back chairs, ash and rush",
   "category": "chairs",
   "image": "chair2.jpg",
   "excerpt": "For a kitchen table in Deventer. Steam-bent back legs, hand-woven rush seats.",
   "date": "2026-09-05",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">The drawing we worked from, a 1937 study of a country ladder-back, belongs to the owner's grandmother. We kept the proportions and made the back slats a little wider for modern backs.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Timber</td><td>Ash from a dieback felling near Lochem</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Rush, woven by Hanneke Vos in Epe</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>Linseed oil</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>3 weeks a chair</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>€1,150 each</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} -->\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cut list</h4>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"className\":\"is-style-paper\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-paper\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>No.</th><th>Timber</th><th>Finished size (mm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Back legs, steam-bent</td><td>2</td><td>Ash</td><td>1020 × 38 × 38</td></tr><tr><td>Front legs</td><td>2</td><td>Ash</td><td>450 × 38 × 38</td></tr><tr><td>Back slats</td><td>4</td><td>Ash</td><td>400 × 60 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>Stretchers</td><td>8</td><td>Ash</td><td>420 × 22 dia.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Rocking chair, cherry",
   "category": "chairs",
   "image": "chair.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A Shaker-pattern rocker with a woven tape seat.",
   "date": "2026-07-18",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Kofi drew it full size on a sheet of plywood first, rockers included, because a rocker that is a few degrees off tips you out or rocks you to sleep too fast.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Timber</td><td>Cherry from a sawmill near Gent</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Cotton tape, woven in the workshop</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>Hardwax oil</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>5 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>€2,300</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} -->\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cut list</h4>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"className\":\"is-style-paper\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-paper\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>No.</th><th>Timber</th><th>Finished size (mm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rockers</td><td>2</td><td>Cherry</td><td>820 × 110 × 28</td></tr><tr><td>Back posts</td><td>2</td><td>Cherry</td><td>1080 × 38 × 38</td></tr><tr><td>Slats</td><td>3</td><td>Cherry</td><td>390 × 70 × 9</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Joined chest, after a 1670s original",
   "category": "cabinets-and-chests",
   "image": "chest.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A copy of a carved oak chest the client saw in a museum.",
   "date": "2026-05-30",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">The client fell for a 17th-century chest from Ipswich, Massachusetts, in a museum and asked whether it could be made again. It could, slowly: the carving alone took Sanne four weeks. The photo shows the original.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Timber</td><td>Riven oak from a single Achterhoek log</td></tr><tr><td>Joints</td><td>Mortise and tenon, pegged, no glue</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>Linseed oil and wax</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>12 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>€7,800</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":4} -->\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cut list</h4>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table {\"className\":\"is-style-paper\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table is-style-paper\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>No.</th><th>Timber</th><th>Finished size (mm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stiles</td><td>4</td><td>Oak, riven</td><td>760 × 90 × 45</td></tr><tr><td>Rails</td><td>6</td><td>Oak</td><td>1150 × 100 × 28</td></tr><tr><td>Panels</td><td>6</td><td>Oak, riven</td><td>360 × 280 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>Lid boards</td><td>2</td><td>Oak</td><td>1250 × 270 × 20</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Piano stool, rebuilt",
   "category": "repairs",
   "image": "stool.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A 1900s adjustable piano stool with a split top and a stripped thread.",
   "date": "2026-04-12",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">We take in one or two repairs a season, when the piece deserves it. This one needed a new walnut top glued up from three boards and a new wooden screw cut on the lathe.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Work</td><td>New top, new screw thread, legs reglued</td></tr><tr><td>Timber</td><td>Walnut for the top</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>Shellac, as the original</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>€480</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "A glue-up, in pictures",
   "category": "workshop-notes",
   "image": "clamps.jpg",
   "excerpt": "Twelve clamps, a timer and ten minutes before the glue skins over.",
   "date": "2026-03-20",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Gluing up a door frame or a table top is the one part of the job where nobody talks. Everything is dry-fitted twice, clamps are set to width, and then it is ten minutes of work and a day of waiting.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Glue</td><td>Hide glue for chairs, so they can be taken apart; PVA for tops</td></tr><tr><td>Clamps</td><td>We own 64 and it is never enough</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "The cupboard in Nuremberg",
   "category": "workshop-notes",
   "image": "cabinet.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A study trip to look at an ash and oak cupboard from about 1600.",
   "date": "2026-02-02",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Sanne spent two days in the Fembohaus in Nuremberg drawing this cupboard. The panels float in their frames exactly as ours do. Four hundred years later, none of them has split.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Where</td><td>Stadtmuseum Fembohaus, Nuremberg</td></tr><tr><td>Date of the cupboard</td><td>About 1600</td></tr><tr><td>Timber</td><td>Ash and oak</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Buying elm on the IJssel",
   "category": "workshop-notes",
   "image": "timber.jpg",
   "excerpt": "How a diseased tree becomes a chair seat, and why it takes three years.",
   "date": "2026-01-10",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">Every winter the council fells the elms that died of Dutch elm disease over the summer. We buy the best butts, have them milled at a sawmill in Voorst and stack the boards in the shed. The photo is a much bigger yard, a century ago.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Bought this winter</td><td>4 logs, about 6 cubic metres</td></tr><tr><td>Ready to use</td><td>Winter 2029</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why we still cut dovetails by hand",
   "category": "workshop-notes",
   "image": "shavings.jpg",
   "excerpt": "A router is faster. We use one for grooves. Drawers get hand-cut dovetails.",
   "date": "2025-11-15",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">A hand-cut dovetail can be spaced to suit the drawer, and the pins can be as thin as a pencil line. It takes Sanne about 40 minutes a corner. We think drawers are the one place people really look.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td>Time per drawer</td><td>About three hours, four corners</td></tr><tr><td>Tools</td><td>A dovetail saw, chisels, a marking gauge and a block plane</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<!-- /wp:table -->"
  }
 ],
